                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_81\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit's diff to core/src/de/gg/util/TickCounter.java removes the line\n`tickCount = maxTicks;` from the TickCounter constructor and updates the\nconstructor's javadoc (dropping \"Has to be reset() first to get used.\").\n\nThat exact line was introduced in candidate_81, which added TickCounter.java\nas a brand-new file (replacing GameSession's previous ad-hoc tick/time-delta\nfields with this new TickCounter abstraction). In that version the\nconstructor set `tickCount = maxTicks` right away:\n\n    public TickCounter(TickHandler tickHandler, int maxTicks, int tickDuration) {\n        this.tickHandler = tickHandler;\n        this.maxTicks = maxTicks;\n        this.tickDuration = tickDuration;\n\n        tickCount = maxTicks;   // <-- the bug\n    }\n\nThis is a genuine logic bug: because `update()` only ticks while\n`tickCount < maxTicks`, and immediately checks\n`if (tickCount == maxTicks) { if (!finished) { finished = true; return true; } }`,\na freshly constructed TickCounter (as created in GameSession.init()) would\nreport the round as immediately finished on the very first `update()` call,\nwithout ever processing any ticks \u2014 the round would end instantly instead of\nrunning for its full duration. The fix removes the erroneous initialization\nso `tickCount` correctly starts at 0.\n\nNote: candidate_82's diff is byte-for-byte identical to fix_commit_diff.txt,\ni.e. it IS the fix commit rather than a distinct candidate change, so it was\nexcluded from consideration as the bug-introducing commit. All other\ncandidates either predate the TickCounter class entirely or only contain\nunrelated changes, so candidate_81 is the sole commit where the buggy\n`tickCount = maxTicks;` initialization was introduced.\n"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
